 "deepDive": {
  "domain": "Culture, media & sport",
  "title": "Who runs Kraków's Music Centre? A one-year truce and an open question",
  "body": "**Analysis.** Kraków's new Music Centre (Centrum Muzyki) next to Cichy Kącik is the biggest cultural building the city has under way. It has become an early test of how Mayor Łukasz Gibała's administration weighs culture against a strained budget. City Hall now expects the opening in **spring 2027**. At a [public hearing on 5 November](https://krakow.pl/aktualnosci/344314,33,komunikat,wysluchanie_publiczne_dotyczace_modelu_zarzadzania_centrum_muzyki_w_krakowie.html), Deputy Mayor Jan Hoffman said 2027 would be a transitional year. The building stays with the municipal development agency ARMK, and the two city orchestras, Capella Cracoviensis and Sinfonietta Cracovia, rent space there with extra city subsidies.\n\nThe disagreement is about what comes after 2027. ARMK president Katarzyna Olesiak argued that the company should keep running the building, with the orchestras setting the programme and a joint committee approving the calendar. The orchestra directors, Rafał Kokot and Jan Tomasz Adamus, want a cultural institution to run it rather than a company. Adamus warned that the one-year arrangement should not turn into an \"extravagant experiment\", [LoveKraków reported](https://lovekrakow.pl/zejdzcie-na-ziemie-zderzenie-dwoch-wizji-centrum-muzyki).\n\n**Money is the main issue.** ARMK puts annual running costs at 15m zł. Some residents at the hearing said that figure leaves out construction loan costs. One speaker put the real cost nearer 50m zł, and that estimate has not been verified. Councillor Michał Starobrat, from the mayor's club, said the centre will never cover its costs. He cited a debt ratio above 95% and warned of a possible recovery programme in 2028. Critics noted that the orchestras gave few figures, while former culture plenipotentiary Paweł Szczepanik said culture is an investment, not a cost.\n\n**What to watch.** On 18 November, Hoffman confirmed to the council that ARMK will run the building in 2027. Culture committee chair Mazurkiewicz's draft resolution asking the mayor to leave the centre with cultural institutions has not yet been voted on. The 2027 budget debate, alongside proposals to leave city memberships and set new 2027 tax rates, will show how much money the orchestras' rent subsidies get. It will also show whether the transitional model becomes the permanent one.",
